  "verbalization": "Cyanide is shown to have a target relationship with the High-affinity choline transporter, which in turn is targeted by multiple compounds, including [(1S,2R,4S,5R,8R,10S,13R,14R,17S,18R)-2-hydroxy-4,5,9,9,13,20,20-heptamethyl-24-oxahexacyclo[15.5.2.01,18.04,17.05,14.08,13]tetracosan-10-yl] acetate, 5,7-Dihydroxy-8-(3-Methylbutanoyl)-6-(3-Methylbut-2-Enyl)-4-Propylchromen-2-One, and Pseudopterosin Q. All of these compounds are linked to the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14, suggesting a potential mechanism where Cyanide may act through a common pathway involving the choline transporter and the proteasome. The evidence indicates that Cyanide could target the choline transporter, which is a component of the proteasome pathway, and that this targeting leads to a downstream effect on the proteasome. The presence of multiple compounds with similar target relationships supports the idea that the choline transporter is a key mediator in this interaction, and that the proteasome may be involved in the cellular response to Cyanide. The integration of these pathways suggests a possible role for the choline transporter in the cellular response to Cyanide, with the proteasome possibly being a downstream effector.",
